  "slug": "quantum-circuit-compilation-0208",
  "summary": "Compilers now optimize atom transport, pulse latency and routing directly, as QEC simulators add ion heating, coherent noise and mid-circuit measurement.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is compilation scored by platform-level cost rather than abstract gate count. NEAT optimizes AOD-based atom transport, placement, routing and scheduling for neutral-atom arrays. SCOPE uses an operator Schmidt time surrogate to cut pulse execution latency and reports gains over EPOC on 104 benchmarks. The quspect SDK selects routings after coupling-constrained resynthesis, using 0.616x the two-qubit gates of Qiskit O3 on 55 circuits. CAVQS and a persistent-occupancy compiler for independent-set problems add synthesis and constraint-preserving structure. A second thread makes QEC simulation more hardware-realistic. THeatA tracks per-ion phonon numbers under QCCD transport. Compiled Clifford-augmented MPS reach distance-9 surface codes under coherent noise. MASQ provides exact near-Clifford sampling, and pre-trajectory GPU sampling now handles general Kraus channels and mid-circuit measurements. GEARS uses syndrome feedback with gate-error amplification for in-situ recalibration, reducing measurement needs about eightfold.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
